Now I have the full picture! Let me trace through exactly what happens.

In `Daemon::run()` (lines 101-123):
```cpp
while (true) {
    wl_display_flush(m_waylandDisplay);
    int _ = poll(fds, 2, -1);
    if ((fds[0].revents & POLLIN) != 0) {
      wl_display_dispatch(m_waylandDisplay);
    }
    if ((fds[1].revents & POLLIN) != 0) {
      ipc.serverAccept(*this);
    }
}
```

This is a single-threaded event loop where `poll()` waits for either Wayland events or an IPC connection, then processes whichever arrives. When `IPC::serverAccept()` runs, it calls `setWallpaper()` synchronously, which for images triggers a full software JPEG decode via `decodeSingleFrameNV12()` — this CPU-bound operation blocks the entire event loop until completion.

For videos, `bind()` just initializes the FFmpeg context without decoding frames; the actual decoding happens lazily during rendering when `decodeNextFrame()` is called. So the critical difference is that images block immediately while videos defer the work.

Looking at the execution flow: when the shell backgrounds two `yinctl` commands, they both queue IPC requests that the daemon processes sequentially through `poll()` and `serverAccept()`, meaning the first image decode blocks before the second request can even be handled. was waiting while the setup stayed fast) → `setWallpaper()` → bind → returns quickly. Both monitors now have their video wallpaper configured and will render their first frames on the next compositor callback, with the actual frame decode happening on-demand during that render cycle, so both decode and render nearly simultaneously. Even if there's minor stagger with videos, it's much less noticeable because the `bind()` call itself is fast and doesn't block, plus the animated transitions visually mask any timing differences. So the core issue really does come down to how yin handles JPEGs versus MP4s — JPEGs force a synchronous full decode during `bind()`, while videos defer that work until the render cycle.

Looking at the actual code, I can see the distinction: for static images that won't be GPU-decoded, there's a check that triggers a single-frame decode path, whereas video formats skip that blocking step entirely. The fix could be making the transition animated to hide the stagger, properly implementing async image decoding, or batching multi-monitor commands into a single IPC request.

