Assistant: Now I see the actual bug. Look at `resumeTransition()` lines 329-337:

```cpp
if (progress >= 1.0f) {
    m_transitionState = nullptr;
}
// called every frame, not just when done
if (m_wallpaper->m_isSingleFrame) {
    render();  // ← calls resumeTransition() again if m_transitionState != nullptr!
}
```

When `progress < 1.0`, `m_transitionState` is still set, so the recursive `render()` hits `m_transitionState != nullptr` and calls `resumeTransition()` again — which calls `render()` again — spinning through the ENTIRE transition in one tight CPU/GPU loop without ever returning to the event loop. Monitor 1 completes its whole transition before monitor 2 gets a single frame callback.

The fix: move the `render()` call inside the `progress >= 1.0` block so it only runs once, after the transition is done:
